item.text:
{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The STDO Axiomatic Calculus for Governed Symbolic Systems, written a_c, is a domain-specific, carrier-neutral constitutional calculus for representing and relating governed symbolic systems. Its stable concept identity is urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:a-c, owned by this standard in urn:stdo:bounded-context:axiomatic-calculus. It is a separately identifiable specialization of STDO principles; its definitions do not depend on a derivation-source record layout, selected subject interpretation, carrier, provider, runtime, or repository topology.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1,
          "quote": "# The STDO Axiomatic Calculus for Governed Symbolic Systems",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems",
          "start_line": 1
        },
        {
          "end_line": 7,
          "quote": "The **STDO Axiomatic Calculus for Governed Symbolic Systems**, written `a_c`,\nis STDO's domain-specific, carrier-neutral constitutional calculus for\nrepresenting and relating governed symbolic systems.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FPosition",
          "start_line": 5
        }
      ],
      "key": "axiomatic_calculus",
      "label": "STDO Axiomatic Calculus"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The calculus's historical design provenance comprises: scoped identity and authority conservation from IDENTITY_METHOD core-law and authority-identity-and-conservation-stdo-up-004; bounded-context meaning and explicit cross-context relations from SPEC_METHOD bounded-context-semantic-resolution; bounded probabilistic evaluation from REFERENCE_FRAME_METHOD position and evaluation; delegated construction, exact candidates, and construction/assessment separation from SPEC_METHOD agentic-construction-execution-stdo-up-020; governed ambiguity, bounded processing, human adjudication, and hard stops from SPEC_METHOD ambiguity-governance-rule; exact basis, authority conservation, evidence/verdict separation, and closed results from REFERENCE_FRAME_METHOD RF-005, RF-006, RF-007, and RF-012; constitutional derivation and re-entry from SPEC_METHOD constitutional-chain; finite material closure, projection, residuals, authority conservation, and capability fit from REFERENCE_FRAME_METHOD reference-frame-laws; and singular released authority from SPEC_METHOD one-constitutional-surface-and-version-boundary-stdo-surface-001. These are provenance references, not an implicit representation or implementation dependency.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 75,
          "quote": "The following clause families document the design provenance of the calculus:\n\n- scoped identity and authority conservation from\n  [`IDENTITY_METHOD.md#core-law`](IDENTITY_METHOD.md#core-law) and\n  [`IDENTITY_METHOD.md#authority-identity-and-conservation-stdo-up-004`](IDENTITY_METHOD.md#authority-identity-and-conservation-stdo-up-004);",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FDerivation%20Provenance",
          "start_line": 71
        }
      ],
      "key": "derivation_provenance",
      "label": "Derivation provenance"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Sigma is the tuple (RecordKind, Sort, RelationKind, ConstraintKind, ResidualKind, FunctorKind, JudgmentKind, StopKind). Every member is a finite closed identity set for one model family. The fixed RecordKind_ac identities use the prefix urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:record-kind: with suffixes semantic-object, typed-relation, constraint, latitude, residual, traversal, transformation, and judgment. The signature specifies fields and value domains; identity-reference domains and cardinalities; relation endpoint sorts; constraint subject/predicate domains; residual subject, uncertainty, consequence and re-entry domains; judgment input, output, evidence and stop contracts; and permitted traversal functor kinds.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 159,
          "quote": "Sigma = (\n  RecordKind,\n  Sort,\n  RelationKind,\n  ConstraintKind,\n  ResidualKind,\n  FunctorKind,\n  JudgmentKind,\n  StopKind\n)",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FCore%20Signature",
          "start_line": 150
        }
      ],
      "key": "core_signature",
      "label": "Core signature"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An exact-basis model is M_b = (b, I, O, E, C, L, X, V, T, J). I is its closed identity universe. Population_M maps the eight core kinds respectively to finite populations of typed semantic objects O, directed typed relations E, constitutional constraints C, declared latitude L, residual uncertainty X, traversals V, transformations T, and judgments J. Local_M is their disjoint union; I = Local_M disjoint_union External_M. RefDomain_Sigma(record_kind, field) returns cardinality, allowed local record kinds, semantic-object sorts, external target kinds, and required basis relation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 201,
          "quote": "M_b = (b, I, O, E, C, L, X, V, T, J)",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FCore%20Signature",
          "start_line": 201
        }
      ],
      "key": "axiomatic_model",
      "label": "Exact-basis axiomatic model"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "SemanticObject has exactly id, sort, context, owner, scope, basis, and value. The first five named identity coordinates id, sort, context, owner, and basis are identities; scope is the closed governed domain; value is sort-defined carrier-neutral content or a reference to exact content.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 291,
          "quote": "`id`, `sort`, `context`, `owner`, and `basis` are identities. `scope` is the\nclosed governed domain. `value` is sort-defined carrier-neutral content or a\nreference to exact content.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTyped%20Records%2FSemantic%20Object",
          "start_line": 289
        }
      ],
      "key": "semantic_object",
      "label": "SemanticObject"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "TypedRelation has exactly id, kind, source, target, context, owner, scope, basis, and qualifiers. Source and target resolve into I. Qualifiers form the closed kind-specific record of direction, cardinality, preservation, loss, inverse, invalidation, and any other relation semantics required by the signature.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 313,
          "quote": "`source` and `target` resolve to members of `I`. `qualifiers` is the closed\nkind-specific record declaring direction, cardinality, preservation, loss,\ninverse, invalidation, or other relation semantics required by the signature.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTyped%20Records%2FTyped%20Relation",
          "start_line": 311
        }
      ],
      "key": "typed_relation",
      "label": "TypedRelation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Constraint has exactly id, kind, applies_to, predicate, context, owner, scope, basis, judgment_kind, latitude_ref, and refusal. Kind and judgment_kind resolve one declared ConstraintKind and JudgmentKind respectively. Its signature owns the predicate language and judging rule; a predicate not deterministically decidable declares its required probabilistic or human judgment kind rather than claiming machine proof.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 339,
          "quote": "`kind` resolves one declared `ConstraintKind`; `judgment_kind` resolves one\ndeclared `JudgmentKind`. The owning signature defines the predicate language\nand how its result is judged. A predicate that is not deterministically\ndecidable states the required probabilistic or human judgment kind rather than\npretending to be a machine proof.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTyped%20Records%2FConstraint",
          "start_line": 335
        }
      ],
      "key": "constraint_record",
      "label": "Constraint"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Latitude is an explicit permission set within a constraint boundary, with fields id, applies_to, allowed_variation, forbidden_variation, context, owner, scope, basis, and invalidation. Silence supplies no latitude; its grant cannot exceed its owner, scope, basis, or invalidation law.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 343,
          "quote": "Latitude is an explicit permission set inside a constraint boundary:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTyped%20Records%2FLatitude",
          "start_line": 343
        },
        {
          "end_line": 360,
          "quote": "Silence is not latitude. Latitude grants no authority beyond its owner, scope,\nbasis, and invalidation law.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTyped%20Records%2FLatitude",
          "start_line": 359
        }
      ],
      "key": "latitude_record",
      "label": "Latitude"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Residual uncertainty is first-class model content. Residual has fields id, subject, kind, uncertainty, consequence, context, owner, scope, basis, re_entry, and invalidation. Kind resolves one declared ResidualKind; its owning signature defines lawful subject, uncertainty, consequence, re-entry and invalidation domains. An undeclared kind or value refuses structural admission.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 388,
          "quote": "`kind` resolves one declared `ResidualKind`. The owning signature defines its\nlawful subject, uncertainty, consequence, re-entry, and invalidation domains.\nAn undeclared residual kind or value refuses structural admission.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTyped%20Records%2FResidual",
          "start_line": 386
        }
      ],
      "key": "residual_record",
      "label": "Residual"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "Z, R, required-edge semantics, basis and scope are exact.",
        "The fixed point is finite and unique."
      ],
      "definition": "Material closure is closure_R(M, Z) = lfp(lambda Q: Z union required_R(M, Q)). It is the least lawful record closure under the exact dependency relation family R; it makes no global minimality claim about prose, bytes, tokens, cost, or cognitive effort. Failure to establish its defining conditions returns a gap or refusal.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 531,
          "quote": "The result is defined only when `Z`, `R`, required-edge semantics, basis, and\nscope are exact and the fixed point is finite and unique. Failure to establish\nthose conditions returns a gap or refusal. Least closure means least lawful\nrecord closure under `R`; it does not claim globally minimal prose, bytes,\ntokens, cost, or cognitive effort.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FFundamental%20Laws%2FAC-010%20Material%20Closure",
          "start_line": 527
        }
      ],
      "key": "material_closure",
      "label": "Material closure"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "A declared consumer task and exact model, seed set and dependency family are bound."
      ],
      "definition": "Projection selects an exact view without changing selected meaning: project(M, Z, R) -> (M_Z, Boundary_Z) | gap | refusal. M_Z contains material closure; Boundary_Z records excluded, external, unresolved, translated and invalidation-sensitive seams and binds the shared faithful-projection support/loss boundary. Carrier or capability budgets do not permit removal of required members.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 546,
          "quote": "`M_Z` contains AC-010's material closure of `Z` under `R`. `Boundary_Z` records\nexcluded, external, unresolved, translated and invalidation-sensitive seams.\nThose coordinates bind the shared contract's support and loss boundary; a\ncarrier or capability budget supplies no permission to trim required members.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FFundamental%20Laws%2FAC-011%20Projection",
          "start_line": 543
        }
      ],
      "key": "model_projection",
      "label": "Model projection"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An overlay adds explicit records to one or more declared Population_M families without mutating its basis model. Override, disambiguation, equivalence, specialization or translation exists only as a typed relation carrying exact authority, direction, scope, basis, preservation, loss, refusal and invalidation semantics.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 554,
          "quote": "An overlay adds explicit records to one or more declared `Population_M`\nfamilies of a basis model. It does not mutate the basis. Override,\ndisambiguation, equivalence, specialization, or translation exists only as a\ntyped relation with exact authority, direction, scope, basis, preservation,\nloss, refusal, and invalidation semantics.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FFundamental%20Laws%2FAC-012%20Overlay",
          "start_line": 550
        }
      ],
      "key": "model_overlay",
      "label": "Overlay"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Transformation t is an operation-bearing specialization of one exact traversal v. Its fields are id, traversal, domain_model, codomain_model, context, owner, scope, basis, operation_authority, preconditions, preservation_relation, preserved, introduced, removed, external_preserved, external_introduced, external_removed, external_resolution_witnesses, residuals, evidence, provenance, stop_states, invalidation and re_entry. T[t](M_b, inputs) returns a proposed successor M_b_prime*, hold, gap or refusal. It declares every permitted mutation, leaves the input unchanged, and neither accepts nor admits its successor.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 559,
          "quote": "A transformation is an operation-bearing specialization of one exact traversal\nrecord `v`:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FFundamental%20Laws%2FAC-013%20Transformation",
          "start_line": 558
        },
        {
          "end_line": 726,
          "quote": "The exact transformation identity `t` declares every mutation it may perform.\nThe input model remains unchanged; `M_b_prime*` is a proposed successor. A\nproposed successor does not accept or admit itself.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FFundamental%20Laws%2FAC-013%20Transformation",
          "start_line": 724
        }
      ],
      "key": "transformation_contract",
      "label": "Transformation contract"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A judgment retains its predicate and unchanged subject. Judgment fields are id, kind, subject, subject_digest, context, owner, scope, basis, evaluator, authority, predicate, verdict, decision, performance, effects, evidence, residuals, provenance and decided_at. Foundations owns result semantics; the interpretation owner binds domain decisions before use. Decision retains the original calculus/domain outcome rather than being another verdict enumeration. Promotion is an explicit relation between an unchanged subject and its admission or acceptance judgment.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 734,
          "quote": "Under F2, F3 and F6, evidence about an exact subject and an authorized decision\nabout it cannot acquire the identity or authority of a changed subject.\nConsequently a judgment retains its predicate and unchanged subject. This\ncalculus chooses the record fields below; Foundations owns their result\nsemantics, and the interpretation owner binds domain decisions before use.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FFundamental%20Laws%2FAC-014%20Judgment%20Separation",
          "start_line": 730
        }
      ],
      "key": "judgment_contract",
      "label": "Judgment contract"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A traversal v is a typed contract with id, domain, codomain, context, owner, scope, basis, preconditions, postconditions, authority, evidence, provenance and stop_states. F_K[v](X_v) -> Y_v | Omega_v binds one functor kind, an exact traversal identity, typed input/result domains and a closed stop refinement. Every result or stop maps to one core verdict on an explicit predicate while retaining the original outcome. Its domain handles graceful refusal, uncertainty, recovery or termination dispositions for undecidable observations; total handling does not prove termination of an external actor or world.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 840,
          "quote": "where `F_K` is one functor kind, `v` is one exact traversal identity, `X_v` and\n`Y_v` are its typed input and result domains, and `Omega_v` is its closed stop\nrefinement. The traversal binds each outcome in `Y_v` or `Omega_v` to one core\nverdict on an explicit predicate and preserves the original outcome. Its\ndeclared domain includes graceful refusal, uncertainty and recovery or\ntermination dispositions for observations it cannot decide. This total\nhandling contract does not prove an external actor or world will terminate.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTraversals%20And%20Functor%20Kinds",
          "start_line": 834
        }
      ],
      "key": "traversal_contract",
      "label": "Traversal contract"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The calculus selects three generic classification identities: F_D = urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:f-d for deterministic evaluation/proof returning judgment without performing the construction judged; F_P = urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:f-p for bounded probabilistic interpretation, construction or proposal; F_H = urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:f-h for explicit human adjudication under exact identity, authority grant, subject, basis and decision contract. Useful or repeatable F_P output acquires no semantic, admission, acceptance or closure authority; human presence grants no ambient authority. These kinds are distinct from actors, executors and domain operations such as Encode, Interpret, Serialize and Measure. Shared kind does not imply shared traversal identity, semantics, inputs, outputs, authority or evidence.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 851,
          "quote": "- `F_D` classifies deterministic evaluation or proof over declared properties.\n  It returns a judgment and does not perform the domain construction being\n  judged.\n- `F_P` classifies bounded probabilistic interpretation, construction, or\n  proposal under an exact traversal contract. Its result has no semantic,\n  admission, acceptance, or closure authority merely because it is useful or\n  repeatable.\n- `F_H` classifies explicit human adjudication under an exact identity,\n  authority grant, subject, basis, and decision contract. Human presence grants\n  no ambient authority.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTraversals%20And%20Functor%20Kinds",
          "start_line": 842
        }
      ],
      "key": "functor_kinds",
      "label": "F_D, F_P and F_H"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Interpret_a_c(subject_basis, signature, interpretation_contract) returns M_subject*, hold, gap or refusal; the asterisk denotes a proposed model without applicable semantic acceptance. Its contract declares exact calculus basis; exact subject identity, inventory and bytes or semantic addresses; signature and compatible extension; mappings to every applicable population; interpreter identity, capability, provenance and stops; structural evaluation; semantic selection and acceptance authority; source re-entry and invalidation. The owner selects subject, signature, material declarations and admitted loss while competent source and acceptance owners retain their decisions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 920,
          "quote": "Interpret_a_c(subject_basis, signature, interpretation_contract)\n  -> M_subject* | hold | gap | refusal\n```\n\nThe trailing `*` marks a proposed model that has not received the applicable\nsemantic acceptance judgment.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FInterpretation%20And%20Models",
          "start_line": 915
        }
      ],
      "key": "subject_interpretation",
      "label": "Subject interpretation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "a_c.X is exact subject X interpreted as a model of a_c. Its content identity is distinct from the calculus, its carrier and its external semantic-acceptance judgment J_X. Acceptance returns accepted or refuse over unchanged M_X.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1040,
          "quote": "a_c       = the pure calculus\na_c.X     = subject X interpreted as a model of a_c\na_c.X.C   = that accepted model encoded in carrier C",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FSubject%20And%20Carrier%20Boundaries",
          "start_line": 1038
        }
      ],
      "key": "interpreted_subject_model",
      "label": "Interpreted subject layer a_c.X"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "The exact interpreted model has the applicable accepted semantic judgment."
      ],
      "definition": "a_c.X.C is that accepted model encoded in carrier C. G_C = Encode_C(M_X, J_X) is followed by the separately bound deterministic carrier-admission judgment D_C = F_D[v_carrier_admission](G_C, Profile_C, CarrierBasis_C), returning admitted or refuse over unchanged carrier bytes. The admitted evidence relation is the exact triple (G_C, J_X, D_C), with neither judgment part of its subject's identity.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1058,
          "quote": "`J_X` is an external semantic-acceptance judgment over unchanged `M_X`.\nEncoding requires `J_X.decision = accepted` and exact equality between the\njudgment subject identity and digest and `M_X`; hold or refusal cannot be\nencoded. `D_C` is a judgment over unchanged carrier bytes. It is not a\ntransformed carrier. The admitted encoding evidence relation is the exact\ntriple `(G_C, J_X, D_C)`; neither judgment enters the identity of its subject.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FSubject%20And%20Carrier%20Boundaries",
          "start_line": 1053
        }
      ],
      "key": "encoded_model_carrier",
      "label": "Encoded carrier layer a_c.X.C"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "The publication bytes already exist."
      ],
      "definition": "AxiomaticCalculusBasis is an external released-edition record, distinct from stable concept identity. It has kind stdo.axiomatic-calculus-basis, schema_version 1, concept_identity urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:a-c, derivation_basis containing immutable absolute predecessor release_uri, manifest_sha256 and nonempty absolute semantic-address principle_refs, and publication_basis containing distinct immutable absolute successor release_uri, manifest_sha256, member_uri and member_sha256. Each digest field is sha256: followed by 64 lowercase hexadecimal characters.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1081,
          "quote": "The stable concept identity does not identify one immutable calculus edition.\nAn exact released calculus basis is an external record created after the\npublication bytes exist. It keeps the predecessor authority from which the\nedition was derived distinct from the successor carrier that publishes it:",
