      ],
      "definition": "Ontology is prior semantic-design authority subordinate to constitutional WHAT from intent, Product, requirements and applicable graph/domain method. It comprises entities, identities, relationships, invariants, lifecycle, authority, atomic functions, higher-order composition, effects and projections. Once accepted it governs these meanings without inventing Product meaning. Architecture and accepted design remain structural HOW for topology, interfaces, carrier placement, module boundaries, algorithms and realization. A class, schema, endpoint, command or carrier inventory alone is not Ontology.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 406,
          "quote": "The Ontology does not replace target architecture or module design. Accepted\ndesign remains the structural `HOW` for topology, interfaces, carrier placement,\nmodule boundaries, algorithms, and local realization. Implementation and tests\nderive from requirements plus accepted design and must preserve the accepted\nOntology; they are not direct substitutes for either layer.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F4B.%20Ontology-First%20Design%20Rule",
