      ],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 236,
          "quote": "`Population_M` is a total function over `RecordKind_ac`. Every population is\nfinite, every fundamental record belongs to exactly one population, and record\nidentities are unique across their disjoint union. Let `Local_M` be that union.\nThen `I = Local_M disjoint_union External_M`, where every member of\n`External_M` is admitted only through one signature-declared external reference\ndomain and exact resolution relation. A hidden record, an unpopulated extra\nrecord family, an identity occurring in two populations, or an identity in `I`\nwith neither one local record nor one lawful external resolution refuses the\nmodel. A population may be empty only when the selected signature does not\nrequire a member of that record kind.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FCore%20Signature",
          "start_line": 227
        }
      ],
      "key": "closed_signature_population_and_references",
      "statement": "Every signature imports the eight core identities unchanged; specialization can constrain fields, values, references and admissible kinds, but cannot remove, rename, shadow or silently add core families. Unknown fields/kinds require a new signature or explicit compatible extension. Population_M is total, finite and disjoint with globally unique record identities. Hidden records, extra unpopulated families, duplicate population membership or identities lacking exactly one lawful local/external resolution refuse the model. Every identity-bearing scalar or collection member is checked through RefDomain_Sigma; local targets satisfy the exact family and, for semantic objects, sort, while external targets satisfy exactly one declared kind and basis relation. Local/external dual resolution, missing domains, wrong families/sorts, ambiguity and dangling external identities refuse structural admission.",
