    {
      "applies_to_keys": ["prime_semantic_atom"],
      "conditions": [
        "Prime acceptance or Prime-conformance is claimed."
      ],
      "exceptions": [
        "An out-of-domain case may justify proposing another candidate atom for design admission."
      ],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 980,
          "quote": "Every accepted Prime atom declares the admitted domain over which its law\nholds. Within that domain it governs every admitted instance. A later use case\ncomposes or parameterizes the atom; it does not fork, shadow, specialize, or\nreinterpret the atom locally. An out-of-domain case may justify proposing\nanother candidate atom for design admission. An in-domain counterexample\nfalsifies the shared atom and re-enters at that atom's design boundary rather\nthan authorizing a feature-local replacement.\n\nAn atom without a declared admitted domain remains a candidate atom. It cannot\nsupply Prime acceptance or Prime-conformance to a realization projection.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F4B.%20Ontology-First%20Design%20Rule%2FPrime%20Semantic%20Atoms%20And%20Universal%20Domain",
          "start_line": 971
        }
      ],
      "key": "prime_domain_conservation",
      "statement": "Every accepted atom declares its admitted domain and governs all admitted instances there. Later cases compose/parameterize rather than locally fork, shadow, specialize or reinterpret. An in-domain counterexample falsifies the shared atom and re-enters its design boundary, without authorizing local replacement. Domainless atoms remain candidates and cannot supply Prime acceptance/conformance.",
